{"acceptance_criteria":"On Apple TV hardware, focusing any Settings row shows the section headers above and below fully clear of the focus pill. SearchView unaffected (extra top padding is harmless).","description":"mherger round 3 (forum post 1823551, image IMG_2798). The focused-row pill in the Settings tab overruns the section header above it (Library) and below it (About).

Build 9 added .tvSectionHeader() (TVList.swift) but it only pads the header BOTTOM, and it was never applied to SettingsView at all — only SearchView and LibraryShelvesPickerView.

Fix: change .tvSectionHeader() to pad .vertical (both sides) so a header gets top clearance too; apply it to all 5 SettingsView section headers (Server/Player/Audio/Library/About) — convert the Section("X") shorthand to Section { } header: { Text("X").tvSectionHeader() }. Hardware-tune the clearance value (16pt may need to be ~20-24)."}

{"acceptance_criteria":"From the Shelves row, pressing DOWN moves focus into the About section; Version/Build/Server/MAC all reachable and readable.","description":"mherger round 3 (forum post 1823551). In the Settings tab the user cannot navigate below the 'Shelves' row — the About section (Version / Build / Server / Player MAC) is unreachable.

Root: SettingsView.swift:193 — the About rows are marked .focusable(false) ('read-only by design'). 'Shelves' is the last focusable element in the list, so pressing DOWN has nowhere to land and the list never scrolls; About stays below the fold. Build number genuinely unreadable.

Fix: drop .focusable(false) from the About row() helper. A focused read-only row is standard tvOS (Apple's own Settings does it). The rows have no action, so focusing them is harmless — it just lets the list scroll to them."}
